\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The index in a constant flux}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{S} be the Pauli module with #{\Gamma_S=C}, let #{a=1}, and let #{F=\mathbb C}. For
#{n=2} take the field of \ref{fdip-015W}, so that the flux through the torus is
#{w}. For #{N_1=N_2=N} with #{N\in\{6,8\}} and every integer #{w} with #{|w|\le N^2/4},
numerical diagonalization of #{\Gamma K_U} gives #{\tfrac{1}{2}(\nu_{-}-\nu_{+})=-2w},
with every computed absolute eigenvalue at least #{10^{-9}}. The forward hop of
#{K_U} at #{x} in direction #{j} uses #{U_j(x)^{-1}}, as in the
[unitary shifts](fdip-009O). For these two-dimensional fields, using the direct link
for the forward hop and its adjoint for the reverse hop reverses the sign of the
computed index at fixed plaquette orientation. For #{n=4}, take
#{N_1=N_2=N_3=N_4=4} and the Pauli module of dimension #{16}.
Sum the link angles of \ref{fdip-015W} in the planes #{(1,2)} and #{(3,4)},
with fluxes #{m_{12}} and #{m_{34}}. The number equals #{4m_{12}m_{34}}
for #{(m_{12},m_{34})} equal to #{(1,1)}, #{(2,1)}, #{(1,-1)}, #{(3,2)}
and #{(4,2)}. For #{(3,3)} the least computed absolute eigenvalue is positive
and the number is #{0}, whereas the product gives #{36}. For a second field,
sum the link angles of \ref{fdip-015W} in the planes #{(1,2)} and #{(1,3)},
with #{m_{12}=m_{13}=1} and all other plane fluxes zero. Its computed count
is #{0}. These numbers come from the indicated finite grids.}

\p{Lüscher bounds the field tensor of an admissible Abelian lattice field and
shows that its magnetic flux is #{2\pi} times an integer fixed within each admissible
flux sector \citet{Equations (2.9)–(2.11), printed pp. 4–5}{luscher1998abelian}.
His trace formula displays the zero-mode difference #{n_--n_+}; the following text
calls #{n_+-n_-} the index and states its constancy within each magnetic flux sector
\citet{Equation (3.4) and following text, printed p. 10}{luscher1998abelian}.
These are zero-mode counts, distinct from the inertia counts #{\nu_\pm} of
#{\Gamma K_U}. Hernández, Jansen and Lüscher prove exponential locality of
Neuberger's operator when all plaquette holonomies are sufficiently close to the identity
\citet{Section 2.3, Equations (2.15)–(2.16), printed pp. 5–6}{hernandez1998locality}.
For Abelian fields in dimension #{2N} satisfying his bounds, Fujiwara states
#{\operatorname{index}D=(-1)^NQ_N} and studies it numerically through spectral flow
\citet{Equations (6), (12)–(13), printed pp. 3–4}{fujiwara2000spectral}.
He observes that commuting links permit reduction to several #{\mathrm U(1)} fields
\citet{closing paragraph, printed p. 11}{fujiwara2000spectral}.
Fujiwara, Suzuki and Wu relate the lattice topological charge to the winding number
of a smooth #{\mathrm U(1)} bundle built from nonexceptional links
\citet{Abstract, p. 1}{fujiwara2000topological}. These statements are cited, not proved here.}

\p{For two-dimensional scalar fields with plaquette angles in the window of \ref{fdip-019X}, the index equals the signed spin multiplicity times the negative of the Wilson bulk integer times the scalar flux. In the product-plane case, \ref{fdip-01CB} gives the higher-dimensional formula with an explicit angle bound on every scalar plaquette.}
